WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.000
The British soldier,

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.520
all around the globe you'll find him,

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.000
from Gibraltar to Hong Kong.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.280
Everywhere he stands against
the threatening years,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.920
staunch symbol of our common will to order.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.480
Fire!

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:09.480
The Spider's Web:
Britain's Second Empire

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.000
The British Empire,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.520
the largest Empire the world has ever known.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.920
For over 300 years Britain ruled,

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.400
it's armies conquered and its bankers
proclaimed the might of its currency.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.640
But one day it all began to fall apart.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.720
One by one countries declared
their independence from Britain,

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.760
and no amount of force
could reverse the tide.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:41.320
As British elites saw their wealth,
privileges and empire disintegrate,

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.040
they began to search for a new role
in a changing world,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.200
and they found one in finance.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.920
This is a film about how Britain
transformed from a colonial power

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.360
to a modern financial power

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.760
and how this transformation
has shaped the world we live in.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.120
In the days of the British Empire

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.920
the City of London was the world's
biggest global financial centre.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.840
The City of London was the beating
financial heart of the British Empire.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.600
Historians Cain and Hopkins called it
the governor of the imperial engine.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.440
Countries in the empire used to use
the sterling currency

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.560
and the City of London was the financier,

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:45.640
not just inside the sterling zone
but outside the sterling zone as well.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.800
As Britain's Empire declined,
so too did the City of London.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:55.320
Newsreel: One British Army truck is bombed,
killing two soldiers and wounding 12.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.920
As rioting becomes daily
more widespread on Cyprus,

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.640
young people of high school age
take an increasing part in the violence.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:05.320
Here, a group of girls are being dispersed

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.040
after a rock throwing battle
with British Military Police.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.720
With the decline of Empire,

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.720
British commercial interests
across the globe were under threat.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.960
In 1956 Egypt nationalized the Suez Canal.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.200
Newsreel: A new Middle East Crisis arises,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.440
as president Nasser of Egypt
tells a wildly cheering crowd in Alexandria

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.520
that Egypt has seized the
internationally owned Suez Canal.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.920
Newsreel: France and Britain
issue a 12-hour ultimatum.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.920
Within hours of its expiration,

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.640
Britain’s warplanes are
bringing their way to Egypt

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.400
and its Bombers attack
5 key cities including Cairo.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.720
The United States was
opposed to the invasion

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:55.440
and put pressure on Britain and France
to withdraw their troops.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.600
There will be no United States
involvement in these present hostilities.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.920
It is our hope and intent that
this matter will be brought before

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.720
the United Nations General Assembly.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.040
There, the opinion of the world
can be brought to bear

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.800
in our quest for a just end
to this tormenting problem.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.520
Britain was humiliated.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.920
The Suez Crisis signified
the end of Britain's role

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.000
as one of the world's major powers.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.400
Following the crisis there was
a run on sterling, the UK pound.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.640
Some suspected the American government

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:36.400
was encouraging this run on the UK pound.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.320
As financiers withdrew
their money from Britain,

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.920
the value of the pound decreased.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:50.000
To protect the value of the pound,
Britain limited the banks overseas lending.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.400
They were unable to invest abroad,

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.920
and obviously they were unhappy.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.640
We don’t know exactly the context,

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.400
but it is very clear that the banks
or their representatives

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.160
made a representation to the Bank of England,

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.400
which in itself was dominated by
representatives from the banking industry.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:14.160
It seems that they reached an agreement,
which was never written.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.200
that if banks intermediated
between two non-residents,

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.160
in a foreign currency,
in that case the dollar.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.120
This particular intermediation,
this particular deal

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:31.840
would not be considered by the
Bank of England as under its own jurisdiction.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:38.560
The banks began to create
a market for dollars in London,

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.400
called the London Eurodollar market.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.400
To differentiate these Euromarket activities
from their domestic banking activities,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.320
banks kept two sets of accounts.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:53.720
The Bank of England, the UK regulator,

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:57.440
declared that the London Euromarket accounts
were not in London,

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:59.200
they were elsewhere,

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.160
and therefore it had no responsibility
for regulating them.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.960
It is about providing a legal space

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.120
in which you pretend
activity is taking place.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.000
And the importance of that is

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.560
that you pretend it is not taking place

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:19.000
in the economy where
it really is taking place.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:23.640
So you are taking activity from
the place where it is regulated and taxed,

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:26.320
and pretending that it is happening elsewhere.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.040
Now where, doesn't really matter,
it's just elsewhere.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:33.720
When American banks realized

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:37.440
that London offered the ability
to avoid US regulations,

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:41.200
they moved their international
operations to the City.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:48.320
Around the same time as American banks

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:51.520
were moving their international
operations to London,

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:55.040
another new kind of financial space
began to emerge,

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.800
far away from London,
in Britain's overseas jurisdictions,

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:01.640
the last remnants of empire.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:05.840
Back in the 1960s the Cayman Islands
was a complete backwater.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.120
The stories go that mosquitos
were so thick in the air

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:12.440
that sometimes they were enough
to suffocate cows.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.600
That is a legend you hear
about the Cayman Islands,

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.160
I don’t know how true it was.
There was nothing happening there.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.400
Accountants and lawyers from London

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:24.560
arrived in the Cayman Islands
and other British dependencies,

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.600
and began to draft a set of
financial secrecy laws and regulations.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:32.560
Because these jurisdictions'
main selling point was secrecy,

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.320
they were called secrecy jurisdictions.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:42.160
What the Cayman Islands was doing,
was straightforward illegal activity.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.320
Drugs money was coming in in huge quantities,

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:49.040
tax evasion, whatever you wanted,
you could have it.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.840
The Bank of England was observing
the developments from London

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:58.200
and noted in a report marked secret
dated 11th of April 1969,

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:03.120
“we need to be quite sure that
the possible proliferation of trust companies,

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:04.840
banks, et cetera,

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:08.400
which in most cases would be
no more than brass plates

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.160
manipulating assets outside the islands,

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.320
does not get out of hand.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:18.360
There is of course no objection to their
providing bolt holes for non-residents,

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:23.000
but we need to be sure that in so doing
opportunities are not created

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:28.760
for the transfer of UK capital to the
non-sterling area outside UK rules.”

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.560
These small territories,
the last remnants of the British Empire,

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.920
which are still overseas territories today,

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:40.200
they are still the last remnants
of the British Empire,

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:45.000
there are 14 of these overseas territories,
seven of them are bona fide tax havens.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.760
Including the Cayman Islands,
Bermuda, the British Virgin Islands,

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.920
some of the biggest tax havens
in the world today are still British.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:57.840
With access to large amounts of
offshore money the Euromarket grew rapidly,

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.120
by 1980 it had reached $500 billion,

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:07.000
by 1988 $4.8 trillion,

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:11.600
and by 1997 nearly 90%
of all international loans

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:14.640
were made through this market.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:20.800
The British Empire had sunk,
leaving hardly a trace behind,

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:24.560
but the City of London
adapted and survived.

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:33.960
The City of London,
London's financial district

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.200
is a peculiar place.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:40.560
It has been called a city within a city,
a state within a state.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.000
It is run by an organisation called
the City of London Corporation,

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:49.240
a private company that performs
all the functions of a local council

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:52.680
with a private police force
and private courts.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:56.800
Those of you who are not from the UK
and some of you even who are,

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:01.520
might not be aware just how weird
a thing the little City of London is

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.680
within the big London.

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:08.440
The City of London is a separate entity
to the wider London,

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:12.400
and it has its own head,
the Lord Mayor,

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:17.120
who is distinct from the mayor
who runs the rest of London.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:23.000
Every November,
the City stages the Lord Mayor's show,

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:26.200
the world’s oldest civic procession.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:41.880
The City of London has long had
this curious legal status,

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.920
because back in 1066 when
William the Conqueror came over,

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:50.160
the City was one of the only portions
of England that he failed to conquer.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.320
And he struck a deal with the City in 1067

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:56.160
that allowed them to continue functioning.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.400
To this day the City of London

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:02.360
is exempt from numerous laws
that govern the rest of Britain.

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:06.120
Its political system derives
from the Middle Ages.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.640
The City's electorate is dominated
not by its residents,

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:13.400
but by the private businesses
operating within the City,

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:17.760
its Lord Mayor is selected
by the heads of medieval guilds.

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:24.400
They have a representative
in the House of Commons,

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.200
called the Remembrancer,

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:29.520
apart from the clerks of the court
of the House of Commons

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:31.760
he is the only unelected person there.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:35.200
All other lobbyists
have to stop in the lobby.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:39.920
The City of London has a permanent
representative in the House of Commons,

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:43.840
whose role is to report back
to the City of London Corporation

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.600
and to lobby parliament
on behalf of the City.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:53.440
The Corporation of London clearly,
is a unique and interesting phenomena

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.480
that should have attracted
many political scientists,

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:01.240
political economists and economists.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.280
But I don't know of anyone

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.800
who has studied systematically
the Corporation of London

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.560
and its impact on policy,
or economic policy.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:15.120
So we can only surmise,

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.920
some people assume or surmise

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:21.640
that the Corporation of London
is extremely powerful

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.600
and is able in one way or another
to shape British policy,

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:28.920
particularly in financial methods.

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:33.160
Clement Attlee the Prime Minister
after the Second World War

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.840
had something to say about
the City of London Corporation.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:40.840
"Over and over again we have seen
that there is in this country

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:44.560
another power than that
which has its seat at Westminster,

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:49.120
the City of London, a convenient term
for a collection of financial interests

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:53.160
is able to assert itself against
the government of the country.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:57.440
Those who control money
can pursue a policy at home and abroad

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:01.680
contrary to that which has been
decided by the people."

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:12.760
At the heart of the City of London
stands the Bank of England.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.920
The Bank of England
is not just a Central Bank,

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:18.400
it is also a financial regulator.

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:20.640
At the demise of empire

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:23.920
the Bank of England used
this regulatory authority

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:27.160
to help attract the
world's banks to London.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:31.160
In 1972,

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:33.920
the Bank of England issued a license to

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:36.640
the Bank of Credit and
Commerce International,

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:39.640
which set up its head office in London.

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:44.720
Within ten years BCCI grew into
the 7th largest bank in the world.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:49.080
Ten years later BCCI was bankrupt.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:54.280
News: The deputy director of the CIA
Richard Kerr said late today,

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:59.400
that the CIA did use BCCI
to support CIA activities overseas.

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:08.400
But BCCI had not just collaborated with
the world's intelligence services,

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:12.120
it had also engaged in
extensive financial fraud,

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.360
money laundering and terrorist financing.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:19.880
BCCI constituted
international global crime

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:22.920
of a level that boggles the mind.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:27.400
News: BCCI was financing terrorism,
the Bank of England knew it,

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:33.160
but instead of supervising it properly,
it tried to prevent the bank's collapse.

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:35.200
I am saying very directly

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:39.840
that the Bank of England had
sufficient information in front of it

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:43.600
to close BCCI 15 months
earlier than it did,

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:47.840
and millions of depositors
were hurt in that process.

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:54.600
Numerous whistle-blowers from BCCI
had contacted the Bank of England,

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:57.640
yet the Bank of England did nothing.

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:02.720
The Bank of England had plenty of time
to intervene and investigate

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:07.840
but it did not do so because the tradition
at that time which still survives,

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:13.200
is that you have to just kind of send
hints and talk over lunch tables

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.640
with the chaps regulating other chaps

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:19.720
and all would be well.

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:22.200
Robin Leigh Pemberton,

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:24.040
the Governor of the Bank of England

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:26.920
at the time of the collapse of BCCI commented,

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:31.360
“The present system of supervision has
served the community well…

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:35.600
If we closed down a bank every time
we found an incidence of fraud,

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:39.840
you would have rather fewer banks
than we do at the moment.”

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:44.000
London was a place for banks
to engage in business

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:46.360
that was not allowed elsewhere,

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:51.400
where senior bankers did not have to worry
about the consequences of their actions.

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:55.640
This is one of the reasons why today
there are more banks in London

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:58.120
than in any other financial centre.

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:00.360
In Britain nobody goes to Jail,

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:03.520
no bankers go to jail,
they generally don't.

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:05.840
They are a protected species.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:08.920
And that is part of the
offshore business model of the UK,

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:12.160
is to say, bring your money here
we will look after you,

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:15.920
we will not put you in prison,
we will let you do what you want.

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:24.480
Light touch regulation was one way
of attracting business to London,

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:26.600
another was secrecy.

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:28.760
From the 1960s onwards,

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.520
City of London institutions began
to establish offshore branches

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:35.280
in former outposts of the British Empire.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:39.600
Their aim was to create offshore centres
with strong secrecy legislation,

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:43.360
in order to attract capital
from across the globe.

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:45.920
Swiss banking secrecy is the most famous,

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:48.760
You know you put your
money in a Swiss bank

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:51.400
and they promise not to tell anybody.

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:54.640
That is one kind of secrecy.
But another kind of secrecy,

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:57.120
which is very British, is the trust,

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:01.840
and the trust is a very slippery,
complicated and devious mechanism.

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:07.320
Trusts emerged the legends have it
from the time of the Crusades,

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:10.560
when the knights would go off
and fight in foreign lands

00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:14.320
and they would leave their assets
in the care of trusted stewards.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.200
What trusts do ultimately is they play
with the concept of ownership.

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:20.920
Ownership is not such a simple thing.

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:25.400
So the settlor, the knight in this case
would hand over the assets to someone,

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:29.400
who these days would be called
a trustee, it is often a lawyer.

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:32.400
Legally you are separated from those assets,

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:35.400
they are not yours, there is a barrier.

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:37.920
You can't be taxed on them;

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:43.160
nobody is going to find anything about
your connection to these assets.

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:46.120
In Britain's offshore jurisdictions

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:49.520
no qualifications are necessary
to be a trustee,

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:53.240
anyone can set up a trust
and act as a trustee.

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:55.920
There is no registry of trusts,

00:17:56.160 --> 00:18:00.120
there are no bodies to certify
that a trust has been set up.

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:05.120
The only persons who know
about the creation of this agreement

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:07.560
are the trustee and the settlor.

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:10.320
There is no obligation to register it.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:13.920
There is no financial reporting
obligation of trusts.

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:18.640
They are not required to put annual
statements onto account anywhere.

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:23.720
So, trusts are to all intents and
purposes invisible arrangements.

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:28.320
Economist John Christensen
was an economic adviser to

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:31.560
the secrecy jurisdiction of
Jersey for ten years.

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:34.440
We are not talking about a few million.

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:37.160
We are talking now about trillions.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:41.240
Trillions of dollars of capital
which apparently belong to nobody,

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:45.560
for tax purposes and for other purposes,
they belong to nobody.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:50.000
Everything, works of art, gold bullion,
racehorses, cars, real estate,

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:54.760
not just financial assets but a
whole load of non-financial assets

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.920
belong to these trusts

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:01.160
sitting there belonging to nobody.

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:03.920
Now, think that one through,

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:08.640
because we are talking about maybe
as much as $50 trillion of assets

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:11.920
sitting offshore behind
these instruments.

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:15.640
The Cayman Islands are among
several British overseas territories

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:19.120
who have signed a new
information exchange agreement

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:22.360
with Britain and the rest of
Europe to tackle tax evasion.

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:25.600
The countries are now required
to automatically provide details

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:28.920
of the ownership of bank accounts
and how they are used.

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:31.640
Cayman became the first
signatory last week.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:35.000
It is important that the
Cayman Islands should be recognised

00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:39.920
as the first overseas territory to sign
such an agreement with the United Kingdom,

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:43.840
and I think this importantly reflects
the constructive approach

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:46.600
that the Cayman Islands has taken

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:51.360
in delivering our shared objective
of rooting out tax evasion.

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:57.160
The trust lies at the core
of the British secrecy model.

00:19:57.400 --> 00:20:00.600
They don't use banking secrecy,
the Swiss use banking secrecy.

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:04.400
The British of course are only too
willing to kill off banking secrecy

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:07.600
because they will then
capture a larger market share.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:10.160
That is why the Brits are doing this.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:15.600
Trusts are the basic building block
of Anglo-Saxon secrecy

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:20.640
and they form the basis from which
complex offshore structures are created.

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:24.800
Every secrecy jurisdiction offers
a specific set of services,

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:27.400
from trusts to shell companies

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:30.400
to secret bank accounts
and nominee directors.

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:33.440
The combination of these services
into complex structures

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:35.720
spanning multiple jurisdictions

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:38.640
enables the creation of secrecy structures

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:41.600
that are almost impossible to penetrate.

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:46.800
An offshore structure will often
have a trust kind of sitting on top of it.

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:51.560
The trust will be here managing the assets,
kind of controlling the assets.

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:55.720
Underneath that the trust will
own some shell companies,

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:58.760
each one might be in a different jurisdiction.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:03.520
So you might have a trust in one jurisdiction,
whose trustees are somewhere else,

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:06.440
whose beneficiaries are somewhere else,

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:09.920
which owns offshore companies
somewhere else,

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:12.920
each of these companies
might then own assets.

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:15.920
They might own a bank account,
a racehorse, a yacht,

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:18.920
a painting, a portfolio of
shares or whatever.

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:24.040
There are numerous variations of trusts
and offshore secrecy structures.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:27.280
There are offshore lawyers
whose work entails

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:31.040
the creation of ever more complex
and obscure structures.

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:35.920
The aim of these structures is to hide
the identity of the owners of offshore assets

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:40.920
and allow offshore wealth to be
recycled back into global markets.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:44.320
Looking at the amount of money

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:47.400
that is administered from tax havens,

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:52.400
you see that it is a curve going like this.

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:57.400
And all the talking we have been doing
along with other wonderful people

00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:01.400
 fighting against tax havens
has not changed anything.

00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:05.400
It is a world were you go on talking
and go on talking,

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:09.920
"you're interesting me, but I'll go on
doing what I want to do."

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:12.160
We know everything today,

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:14.720
because we have had the Panama Papers,

00:22:14.960 --> 00:22:18.640
we have seen it,
and we are not able to act upon it.

00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:21.400
And we are not able to act upon it,

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:26.040
because this system is really protecting

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:29.920
the few people who are
benefiting from it.

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:36.920
And these are in percentage
very few people,

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:39.160
but they are powerful.

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:43.120
And I see in the Panama Committee
that I'm sitting on

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:46.160
that the only thing that could change this

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:51.240
is to have a publicly accessible
register of the beneficial ownership

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:54.960
of trusts and of all kinds
of companies,

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:58.080
it is easy.

00:23:00.600 --> 00:23:03.480
The Panama Papers are
a collection of leaks

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:06.520
from the offshore law firm
Mossack Fonseca.

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:10.280
Mossack Fonseca is the
4th largest offshore law firm,

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:14.040
the other nine of the ten
largest offshore law firms

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:17.280
are registered in British
overseas jurisdictions.

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:21.520
When countries complain to Britain

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:24.760
about the activities run out of
its offshore havens,

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:27.720
Britain claims that these
places are independent

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:29.920
and that there is nothing it can do.

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:32.920
I have heard time and
time again from officials

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:36.360
in Berlin and in Paris and in
Washington and in other countries,

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:39.720
that they have been told
by the British government,

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:42.960
“yes, they are well aware of
what is going on in Jersey

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:45.200
and they think it is very unfortunate,

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:48.040
but they don't have the
powers to intervene.”

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:50.320
Well, that is a straightforward lie.

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:53.360
They do have the powers to intervene,
they just choose not to.

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:56.040
Britain plays this kind of game

00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:59.160
of pretending when it
suits them to pretend

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:02.240
that these places are independent.

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:05.480
At the end of the day Britain
appoints the governor,

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:08.440
appoints lots of senior
people in these places,

00:24:08.680 --> 00:24:11.560
they are responsible for
foreign relations and defence

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:14.640
and also they can veto
their legislation as well.

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:16.920
So, Britain has a massive degree of control,

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:19.160
basically it is controlling these places.

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:22.320
It is allowing them a little
bit of political space.

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:25.360
During his time as economic
adviser to Jersey,

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:28.400
John Christensen frequently
travelled to London

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:31.440
for talks with various
British government departments.

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:36.280
As economic adviser I had a lot of
contact with different departments.

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:40.120
Traditionally, UK governments
have tried not to interfere

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:43.920
in the domestic affairs
of places like jersey.

00:24:44.160 --> 00:24:47.440
So it happens in a more subtle sort of way,

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:51.920
you kind of go and talk to someone
either at the Home Office or the Treasury

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:53.640
and they would say,

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:57.600
“we are not actually particularly keen
on this piece of legislation,

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:01.040
it might be a good idea if you
didn't go down that route.”

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:04.120
And over a cup of tea that is
quite a strong signal,

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:08.760
that is a signal, “go back to the island
and say they don't want you to do this.”

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:12.800
The British government prefers
not to interfere overtly,

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:16.840
instead, it communicates its
desires through informal discussions.

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:19.920
There is no paper trail or
official statement,

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:23.640
discussions take place
behind closed doors.

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:28.600
The relationship that these
places have with London

00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:31.320
is very much about the British establishment,

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:33.600
people understanding each other.

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:36.920
Anybody who is British or
knows British people

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:40.640
knows that communication
between us is often very subtle

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:45.800
and you have to kind of know
the codes when people say stuff,

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:50.560
there is a lot of irony involved
and a lot of codified language.

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:57.320
People kind of understand how it works.

00:25:57.560 --> 00:26:00.600
And I think that is very much the case

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:04.520
with the British relationship
with the tax havens.

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:09.160
I think there is a lot of understanding
of what we can and can't do

00:26:09.400 --> 00:26:12.920
without anyone having to actually spell it out.

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:15.800
By keeping its power hidden,

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:20.560
Britain is able to claim that these
jurisdictions are politically autonomous.

00:26:23.080 --> 00:26:25.920
Independence for Cayman is not
on the government's agenda,

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:29.520
that is the message that is being sent to the
UN special committee on decolonization.

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:33.120
Attorney Steve Mcfield will be representing
government at the UN meeting,

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:34.960
the committee was set up

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:38.120
to help colonies around the world on the
road to independence,

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:41.000
but Mr Mcfield says
he will be making it clear

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:43.520
that Cayman is not ready for that transition.

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:47.560
And that message is that
the Premier, this Cabinet, this Party,

00:26:47.800 --> 00:26:51.720
has no mandate from the people of
the Cayman Islands to seek independence,

00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:54.920
that is the message that
I will be carrying.

00:26:56.160 --> 00:27:00.320
When the Bahamas declared
independence from Britain in 1967,

00:27:00.560 --> 00:27:04.040
the offshore bankers relocated
to the Cayman Islands

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:06.720
and continued their business from there.

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:09.160
It was the British connection

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:13.920
that reassured bankers and their
clients that their money was safe.

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:15.640
This British bedrock

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:19.400
is what has allowed these
places to become so trusted

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:24.160
by the financial services industry
and offshore finance and all these people.

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:29.360
In reality much of the wealth
administered in Britain's offshore havens

00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:31.520
is controlled from London.

00:27:31.760 --> 00:27:33.560
The City of London by and large

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:36.920
likes to do its really dirty work
outside London.

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:41.640
There might, heaven forbid, be a
regulator who actually takes the job seriously

00:27:41.920 --> 00:27:45.200
and starts to prosecute them
for fraud in London.

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:48.960
So better to do the frauds offshore
in Gibraltar or Jersey

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:53.920
where there is much less risk that a
serious prosecution will ever happen.

00:27:54.760 --> 00:27:57.920
Deals are often discussed
and concluded in London

00:27:58.160 --> 00:28:02.920
but then registered offshore, for tax,
transparency and regulatory purposes.

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:08.160
What they allow the City to do is to
get involved in dirty business

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:10.600
but then when the scandal hits to say,

00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:15.400
"well, they are kind of independent there
is nothing we can do about those places.

00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:19.600
That is not us, that is tax haven
activity and we are the City.

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:22.320
We are not involved in that kind of stuff."

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:25.600
So it is an incredibly convenient relationship.

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:29.480
The City of London has
shaped the way in which

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:33.240
Jersey, Guernsey and the
British overseas territories

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:37.000
have developed as tax havens.

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:41.320
I see these places as
the Frankenstein’s

00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:44.160
created by the City of London.

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:53.800
Today, the UK is the world's largest
provider of international financial services.

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:57.840
The UK has an almost unique role
in global finance.

00:28:58.120 --> 00:29:00.000
If you look at the data,

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:04.920
the kind of data that we have constructed
in the financial secrecy index,

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:08.240
you can see the relative
shares of each country

00:29:08.520 --> 00:29:12.200
in the global provision of
financial services exports,

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:15.440
that is financial services
to non-residents.

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:17.720
Now, there are two big centres,

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:21.440
and everything else is
quite small in comparison.

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:25.280
The two large centres are the United States

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:28.120
with around 19% of the global market,

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:31.840
and the United Kingdom and
its offshore jurisdictions,

00:29:32.120 --> 00:29:35.200
which have around 25% of
the global market.

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:39.240
And if you add to that other jurisdictions,

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:43.000
ex-colonies, but recently
independent, fairly recently,

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:48.760
by which I mean Hong Kong, Singapore,
maybe even Dubai, Bahrain and Cyprus,

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:55.600
then you reach a figure of nearly 40%.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:30:00.120
And I think that figure represents
better the position of London

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:03.840
the position of London in
the global financial market.

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:09.320
The soldiers left,
the administrators left all the colonies,

00:30:09.560 --> 00:30:14.000
but they still kept a significant degree
of control over the financial flows

00:30:14.240 --> 00:30:18.440
from these former parts of
empire and the rest of the world.

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:21.800
So you could describe it
as a second empire,

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:23.920
Britain's second empire,

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:27.920
hidden financial empire
spanning large parts of the globe.

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:32.400
At the time of the British Empire,

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:36.520
the City of London was the
world’s largest global financial centre.

00:30:37.160 --> 00:30:41.240
Not only Britain’s colonies,
but also independent countries

00:30:41.520 --> 00:30:43.800
did their banking in London,

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:47.560
and used the Empire’s currency
for trade and financing.

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:52.800
As the Empire declined,
so too did the City of London.

00:30:54.800 --> 00:30:57.480
The establishment of the
London Euromarket

00:30:57.720 --> 00:31:00.920
enabled City of London banks
to continue to exploit

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:04.040
exploit their Empire era
networks and expertise,

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:06.920
and the creation of secrecy jurisdictions

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:10.440
gave banks access to large
amounts of cheap money.

00:31:10.720 --> 00:31:15.120
International banks from across the
globe set up branches in London and

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:20.120
Britain's offshore jurisdictions in order
to take advantage of this new system.

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:25.080
This system has taken the
place of the occupation.

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:30.320
Up to 1962 for France,
I don't know for England,

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:32.400
but about the same time,

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:37.920
England was physically present in
India for instance or in other colonies.

00:31:40.360 --> 00:31:44.920
And when you look at the money flows
through the tax havens,

00:31:45.160 --> 00:31:49.640
they are increasing when
we withdraw from the colonies.

00:31:51.400 --> 00:31:56.200
We are still plundering developing
countries as former colonial powers.

00:31:59.520 --> 00:32:03.600
Wealthy individuals, organized crime
and corporations

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:08.360
shifted their wealth offshore
in exchange for secrecy and no tax.

00:32:08.600 --> 00:32:10.920
And, as countries around the world

00:32:11.160 --> 00:32:14.240
began to deregulate and
open their economies,

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:16.920
it became ever easier to do so.

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:20.640
Today, as much as half of
all global offshore wealth

00:32:20.920 --> 00:32:24.400
may be hidden in Britain's
secrecy jurisdictions.

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:28.240
One of the losers is Africa,

00:32:28.480 --> 00:32:33.280
whose flight capital flows mostly
into the modern British spider’s web.

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:38.920
I think it is no coincidence that
Britain's offshore empire

00:32:39.160 --> 00:32:41.920
emerged more or less at the same time

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:44.920
as the collapse of the formal empire.

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:48.120
We tend to think of Africa as being

00:32:48.360 --> 00:32:51.280
a huge net debtor to the rest of the world.

00:32:51.520 --> 00:32:54.040
But, that was the extent of their debts

00:32:54.280 --> 00:32:57.760
at the end of 2008, $177 billion.

00:32:59.080 --> 00:33:03.880
The debt of Sub-Saharan African nations
stood at $177 billion in 2008;

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:09.240
yet, the wealth these countries’
elites had moved offshore

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:14.000
between 1970 and 2008 is estimated
at 944 billion dollars,

00:33:15.560 --> 00:33:18.760
over 5 times their foreign debt.

00:33:20.360 --> 00:33:23.640
$944 billion,
do the maths,

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:26.080
far from being a net debtor,

00:33:26.360 --> 00:33:28.360
Africa, sub-Saharan Africa,

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:32.600
is a net creditor to
the rest of the world.

00:33:33.840 --> 00:33:35.840
As capital moved offshore,

00:33:36.120 --> 00:33:40.920
African nations borrowed money from
international banks at high interest.

00:33:41.160 --> 00:33:44.160
Over time, these debts
became so great

00:33:44.400 --> 00:33:46.600
that they may never be repaid.

00:33:47.920 --> 00:33:51.040
Secrecy jurisdictions were
starving developing nations

00:33:51.320 --> 00:33:54.400
of their wealth and
their tax revenues.

00:33:54.640 --> 00:33:57.160
For a long time many
developing countries,

00:33:57.400 --> 00:33:59.120
including recently Ecuador,

00:33:59.360 --> 00:34:03.440
have been trying to set up a UN tax
body which is of a higher standard

00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:07.200
than what is currently there,
which is the UN tax committee,

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:10.960
and every time this has happened
it has been blocked.

00:34:11.200 --> 00:34:14.720
During the Financing for Development
process in Addis Ababa last year,

00:34:14.960 --> 00:34:18.440
I think it was the UK and the US
that again blocked attempts

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:21.200
to set up this world tax organization.

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:25.160
Why would they not want to have
democratic decision making

00:34:25.400 --> 00:34:29.320
in global decisions on how tax
gets collected across borders?

00:34:29.560 --> 00:34:31.560
I don't understand this.

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:34.320
As long as we have cross border activity

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:37.240
that involves both criminal
and legal activity,

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:40.000
as long as we are unable
to separate the two,

00:34:40.240 --> 00:34:43.160
this is going to continue to be a problem.

00:34:44.800 --> 00:34:48.680
Western nations block attempts
to provide greater transparency

00:34:48.920 --> 00:34:50.920
of international financial flows

00:34:51.200 --> 00:34:53.880
and the implementation of
global standards for

00:34:54.120 --> 00:34:56.640
the collection of tax across borders,

00:34:56.880 --> 00:34:59.920
whilst elites in developing
nations use offshore centres

00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:02.640
to hide their wealth offshore.

00:35:02.920 --> 00:35:06.400
The oil in Gabon has not
benefited the people in Gabon.

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:12.160
And this is true for the copper in Zambia,
for the gold in Mozambique or in Mali.

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:21.120
They have benefited the companies
that extract it and their shareholders,

00:35:22.960 --> 00:35:26.760
 it has benefited the corrupt
elite in the country.

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:31.000
In developing countries the
offshore system of tax havens

00:35:31.240 --> 00:35:35.240
has facilitated the looting of
these countries by their elites,

00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:38.320
it has enabled them
to steal the money

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:40.800
and keep it safe somewhere else.

00:35:41.040 --> 00:35:42.920
Illicit financial flows,

00:35:43.160 --> 00:35:46.040
because the anonymity
that drives them

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:50.600
creates incentives for people
in positions of power to be corrupt,

00:35:50.840 --> 00:35:53.640
This is why there is
a group of countries

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:56.600
where development
progress is so difficult.

00:35:56.840 --> 00:36:01.360
It is that the incentives of individuals
and the ability to pursue them

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:05.520
through all the mechanisms
that underpin illicit flows,

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:09.760
outweigh the type of mechanisms
that lead you to a powerful,

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:14.920
 representative, effective state that
can start to deliver development.

00:36:26.160 --> 00:36:30.920
Worldwide, developing countries loose
over a trillion dollars every year

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:34.120
in capital flight and tax evasion.

00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:37.840
Most of this wealth flows into
large western nations

00:36:38.120 --> 00:36:40.320
like the United States and Britain,

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:43.560
and enables their currencies
to stay strong,

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:46.800
whilst developing nations
currencies remain weak.

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:49.560
But illicit flows into western nations

00:36:49.800 --> 00:36:52.920
also had another unexpected side effect,

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:57.920
the economies of the United States
and Britain began to financialise.

00:36:58.160 --> 00:37:02.160
The origins of this financialisation
or de-industrialization

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:05.320
go back to the 1960s.

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:10.080
News: Anti-war demonstrators protest
US involvement in the Vietnam War,

00:37:10.320 --> 00:37:13.240
in mass marches,
rallies and demonstrations.

00:37:13.480 --> 00:37:16.000
President Johnson meanwhile,
let it be known

00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:19.440
that the FBI is closely
watching all anti-war activity.

00:37:19.680 --> 00:37:22.280
In the 1960s US Economist
Michael Hudson

00:37:22.520 --> 00:37:25.720
was working at Chase Manhattan
bank on Wall Street

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:28.640
as Chase's balance of
payments economist.

00:37:28.920 --> 00:37:30.400
During the 1960s,

00:37:30.640 --> 00:37:33.560
the United States
balance of payments deficit

00:37:33.800 --> 00:37:37.920
was entirely a result of
foreign military expenditure.

00:37:38.160 --> 00:37:40.920
Dollars were flowing out
of the United States

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:43.760
as a result of the cost
of the Vietnam War.

00:37:44.000 --> 00:37:46.400
The United States
attempted to prevent

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:50.320
the dollars flowing to Vietnam from
being deposited in foreign banks.

00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:53.920
The government asked Chase to
set up a branch in Saigon

00:37:54.160 --> 00:37:55.640
during the Vietnam War.

00:37:55.920 --> 00:37:58.400
As you can imagine it
didn't have windows,

00:37:58.640 --> 00:38:01.160
it was kind of a fortress,
it lost money.

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:03.920
But the government went
to Chase and said,

00:38:04.160 --> 00:38:07.920
"if you don't get this money that is being
thrown off by the military in Vietnam,

00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:11.920
then it is going to go into French banks,
it will get to general De Gaulle

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:16.120
and you know what he is going to do
every month, he is going to cash it in for gold."

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:19.560
That is what the United States
was trying to stop.

00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:23.520
The US was not successful in
stopping the outflow of money,

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:26.560
so it began to hatch a different plan.

00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:30.000
In 1967, Michael Hudson
was handed a memo

00:38:30.240 --> 00:38:32.560
 by a former State
Department employee.

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:36.520
In 1967 I was given by a former
State Department employee,

00:38:36.760 --> 00:38:39.240
in the elevator at Chase Manhattan,

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:41.920
a memo from the State Department,

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:45.160
urging that Chase Manhattan
would take the lead

00:38:45.400 --> 00:38:50.120
in helping the United States become
the Switzerland of the world,

00:38:50.360 --> 00:38:52.360
meaning for flight capital.

00:38:52.600 --> 00:38:56.120
The State Department through
Chase asked me to estimate,

00:38:56.360 --> 00:39:00.160
"how much money do you think
is available if America were to become

00:39:00.400 --> 00:39:03.120
the new Switzerland and
how do we do it."

00:39:03.360 --> 00:39:06.360
The plan was to organise
offshore banking centres,

00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:08.800
in the Caribbean and elsewhere,

00:39:09.040 --> 00:39:12.560
and that the hot money wouldn't
come directly into Chase

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:15.920
because that wouldn't be
very nice and very legal.

00:39:16.160 --> 00:39:19.240
What happened was, the
Latin American criminals,

00:39:19.520 --> 00:39:23.000
other criminals, drug dealers,
all sorts of organized crime,

00:39:23.240 --> 00:39:26.760
would put their money into the
offshore Caribbean banks

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:30.000
and these offshore banks would then

00:39:30.240 --> 00:39:33.760
deposit the inflow in the
US head office.

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:38.880
By moving offshore dollars
back into the United States,

00:39:39.120 --> 00:39:42.120
the US was able to stop
the outflow of dollars

00:39:42.400 --> 00:39:45.200
and support the
value of its currency.

00:39:46.480 --> 00:39:49.920
Every country looks to
its foreign exchange rate.

00:39:50.160 --> 00:39:53.920
Foreign exchange rate is
not only imports and exports,

00:39:54.160 --> 00:39:56.160
it’s capital movements.

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:59.160
And if you look at the
International Monetary Fund's

00:39:59.400 --> 00:40:01.640
monthly international financial statistics,

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:04.640
you have sort of a steady
balance of trade,

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:07.640
a steady immigrants and remittances,

00:40:07.920 --> 00:40:11.840
what goes up and down are
called errors and omissions.

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:17.120
What the United Nations and the IMF
call errors and omissions are flight capital.

00:40:17.360 --> 00:40:22.160
The reason that it is omitted is that
they don't really like to look at this.

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:27.200
In the 1930s, Roy Ovid Hall, economist
for the US Commerce Department

00:40:27.440 --> 00:40:32.160
wanted to include criminal movements
in the balance of payments statistics.

00:40:32.400 --> 00:40:34.520
Congress got very upset;

00:40:34.760 --> 00:40:37.920
I was told in Washington
the argument was,

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:42.800
"we are a Christian country we
don't want to report crime."

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:47.760
They forbid him to include criminal
money in the balance of payments.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:51.000
I guess now you call it
errors and omissions,

00:40:51.240 --> 00:40:54.760
you don't call it criminal
inflow and outflow.

00:40:56.680 --> 00:40:58.920
In the 1960s and 70s,

00:40:59.160 --> 00:41:02.720
Britain was faced with a similar
dilemma as the United States,

00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:07.000
money was flowing out and this
decreased the value of the pound.

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:11.320
Britain realized that it too could
support the value of its currency

00:41:11.560 --> 00:41:15.440
by opening its domestic market
to the trillions of dollars

00:41:15.720 --> 00:41:18.120
passing through its offshore havens.

00:41:18.360 --> 00:41:22.840
But just as in the United States,
this had an unexpected side effect.

00:41:23.120 --> 00:41:26.600
British banks today,
just as in the 19th century,

00:41:26.840 --> 00:41:29.920
don't put their money into
British manufacturing,

00:41:30.160 --> 00:41:33.160
they put their money into
real estate speculation,

00:41:33.400 --> 00:41:36.920
into financial speculation,
foreign currency trade.

00:41:37.160 --> 00:41:39.160
So, the financialisation of London

00:41:39.400 --> 00:41:42.120
has helped de-industrialise the country,

00:41:42.360 --> 00:41:45.560
because it has enabled
sterling to be supported

00:41:45.800 --> 00:41:48.320
by this huge inflow of hot money,

00:41:48.560 --> 00:41:52.560
this inflow of drug dealing money,
criminal money and tax evasion money

00:41:52.800 --> 00:41:56.320
all over the world that is
going to London

00:41:56.560 --> 00:42:01.040
instead of going to Switzerland,
Lichtenstein or the Caribbean.

00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:09.440
With the silent backing
of the United States,

00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:12.360
Britain’s offshore havens grew rapidly

00:42:12.640 --> 00:42:15.320
and before long the offshore system

00:42:15.560 --> 00:42:19.680
developed into the world’s dominant
international financial market.

00:42:19.920 --> 00:42:23.120
Few were aware how
this market functioned.

00:42:23.360 --> 00:42:27.360
In 1986 Economist John Christensen
went offshore to investigate.

00:42:27.640 --> 00:42:30.920
He applied for a position
at the Jersey office

00:42:31.160 --> 00:42:34.240
of one of the world's
major accounting firms.

00:42:34.520 --> 00:42:36.000
At Deloitte &amp; Touche

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:40.040
I was working in what is called
company and trust administration,

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:42.520
straightforward offshore stuff.

00:42:42.760 --> 00:42:45.640
I went offshore specifically
to work in that area,

00:42:45.920 --> 00:42:49.400
because that is where you're
dealing with the offshore companies,

00:42:49.640 --> 00:42:53.160
the shell companies, the offshore
trusts and you are administering them.

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:56.920
That way I could see from working
inside a big global accounting firm,

00:42:57.160 --> 00:42:59.640
exactly what the clients were doing.

00:42:59.920 --> 00:43:03.400
I had complete access
to all the client files

00:43:03.640 --> 00:43:07.360
and over the course of my period
of working with Deloitte &amp; Touche

00:43:07.600 --> 00:43:11.120
I investigated over 100
of their clients offshore

00:43:12.240 --> 00:43:15.120
and this is what I found.

00:43:16.240 --> 00:43:19.120
There were some insider traders,
some market rigging,

00:43:19.360 --> 00:43:22.880
some avoiding disclosure of conflicts
of interest, illicit arms trading,

00:43:23.120 --> 00:43:26.640
illicit political campaign donations,
contract kickbacks, bribery,

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:30.360
fraudulent invoicing, trade
mispricing and tax evasion.

00:43:31.720 --> 00:43:35.200
This is what the clients were doing.

00:43:37.600 --> 00:43:41.280
On the basis of a sample of
the clients that I looked at,

00:43:41.520 --> 00:43:44.240
not a single client was
involved in what

00:43:44.480 --> 00:43:47.280
I would regard as genuinely
legitimate activity,

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:51.840
they were all involved in some
kind of tax dodging or worse.

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:56.440
I met with Carl Levin,
he used to be an American Senator,

00:43:56.680 --> 00:44:00.600
and he made a lot of inquiries
into private banking.

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:02.920
Asking bankers,

00:44:03.160 --> 00:44:06.840
"what do you think the percentage
is among your clients

00:44:07.120 --> 00:44:10.800
that are using these companies
for legitimate purposes?"

00:44:15.280 --> 00:44:17.280
And the answer was,

00:44:17.520 --> 00:44:20.200
"I believe that 99.9% of my clients

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:23.960
are using these companies
for illicit purposes."

00:44:25.760 --> 00:44:28.240
This is the reality,

00:44:28.480 --> 00:44:32.000
this is American bankers
telling what they are doing,

00:44:32.240 --> 00:44:36.440
and it is exactly the same thing with
UK bankers helping their clients

00:44:36.680 --> 00:44:41.200
to have accounts in Panama or
Bermuda or in the Cayman Islands.

00:44:48.680 --> 00:44:51.360
Secrecy jurisdictions are heavily used

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:54.600
for fraudulent and grey area
financial activities,

00:44:54.880 --> 00:44:58.880
areas were secrecy is not just
desirable, but a necessity.

00:44:59.960 --> 00:45:02.920
Legitimate financial activity has no need

00:45:03.160 --> 00:45:06.000
for the secrecy offshore havens provide,

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:10.760
nor a desire to pay the high fees
offshore banks and law firms charge.

00:45:14.320 --> 00:45:17.800
Today, close to half of the
world's secrecy jurisdictions

00:45:18.040 --> 00:45:20.240
are British dependencies.

00:45:20.520 --> 00:45:24.000
In public, these jurisdictions
claim they are transparent

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:26.440
and their financial services sectors

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:29.200
are engaged in legitimate
financial activities.

00:45:29.440 --> 00:45:33.840
Our economy is not based on secrecy,
it's based on transparency,

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:38.960
it's based on a sound
regulatory environment,

00:45:39.200 --> 00:45:42.840
it's based on good governance
and good government,

00:45:43.120 --> 00:45:46.400
with a British government legal system,

00:45:46.640 --> 00:45:50.360
that is what our financial
institutions are based on.

00:45:51.000 --> 00:45:54.480
Well, tonight we have a response
from the head of Cayman Finance,

00:45:54.720 --> 00:45:58.320
after a group of US-based anti-tax
evasion activists announced plans

00:45:58.600 --> 00:46:02.600
to travel to Cayman to draw attention to
what they feel is corporate tax evasion.

00:46:02.840 --> 00:46:04.920
Finance Chairman Mr Richard Cole says,

00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:07.200
he is encouraging the group to visit

00:46:07.440 --> 00:46:10.320
and says our financial sector
has nothing to hide.

00:46:10.600 --> 00:46:13.480
I would say if anybody
wants to come to Cayman

00:46:13.720 --> 00:46:15.720
to find out what we do here,

00:46:16.000 --> 00:46:19.480
come on down,
we have no secrets here.

00:46:20.680 --> 00:46:24.160
When I went to the Cayman
Islands back in 2008,

00:46:24.400 --> 00:46:27.600
I called the government
spokesman and he said,

00:46:27.880 --> 00:46:30.160
"we have had an order from on high

00:46:30.400 --> 00:46:33.920
that nobody is allowed to speak
to you, you are off-limits."

00:46:34.640 --> 00:46:38.720
In 2011 Journalist Nicholas Shaxson
released “Treasure Islands",

00:46:38.960 --> 00:46:42.880
a ground-breaking book
about the offshore system.

00:46:43.120 --> 00:46:46.240
The author of a series of
international media reports says

00:46:46.480 --> 00:46:49.960
he would welcome a debate with
Cayman Finance chair Tony Travers.

00:46:50.240 --> 00:46:52.520
Mr Travers recently called Nicholas Shaxson

00:46:52.760 --> 00:46:56.080
an imbecile with the
understanding of an 11-year-old.

00:46:56.320 --> 00:46:59.120
There are always going to be
the politics of envy,

00:46:59.360 --> 00:47:02.560
now, the politics of envy are
exacerbated by imbeciles,

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:06.640
who don't actually understand what
is going on in the Cayman Islands.

00:47:06.880 --> 00:47:09.360
I don't know what he's talking about

00:47:09.640 --> 00:47:13.120
and furthermore he doesn't
know what he's talking about.

00:47:14.200 --> 00:47:18.680
The Cayman Islands is the fifth
largest financial centre in the world,

00:47:18.920 --> 00:47:21.440
it hosts 80,000 registered companies,

00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:24.800
over three quarters of the
world's hedge funds,

00:47:25.040 --> 00:47:27.520
and $1.9 trillion in deposits.

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:31.800
It has a population of 60’000,

00:47:32.040 --> 00:47:35.840
roughly equivalent to
New York’s homeless population.

00:47:40.120 --> 00:47:43.920
A strange mixture of characters
populate the offshore world:

00:47:44.160 --> 00:47:46.360
British ex-public school boys,

00:47:46.600 --> 00:47:49.400
members of the world's
intelligence services,

00:47:49.640 --> 00:47:54.160
global criminals, assorted lords
and ladies and bankers galore.

00:47:56.800 --> 00:48:00.920
With so much at stake for so many
wealthy and powerful individuals,

00:48:01.160 --> 00:48:04.640
it may come as no surprise that
Britain's offshore havens

00:48:04.920 --> 00:48:07.800
have developed their own
curious mechanisms

00:48:08.040 --> 00:48:10.760
to prevent information from leaking out.

00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:13.920
For many people who work offshore

00:48:14.160 --> 00:48:16.920
and don't like what they are doing,

00:48:17.160 --> 00:48:19.240
it is very hard indeed to dissent,

00:48:19.520 --> 00:48:23.520
because they will be attacked personally
with a degree of viciousness

00:48:23.760 --> 00:48:25.960
which is quite extraordinary.

00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:31.840
Most of the time the instruments of
suppression are relatively sophisticated,

00:48:32.080 --> 00:48:36.760
"you won’t get promotion by doing this,
your family won’t like it."

00:48:39.160 --> 00:48:41.840
It's not generally, "lets slam them in prison,"

00:48:42.080 --> 00:48:43.800
that is far too crude.

00:48:44.040 --> 00:48:47.560
We are talking about very
substantially the establishment,

00:48:47.800 --> 00:48:50.080
the British establishment in these cases,

00:48:50.360 --> 00:48:52.840
and the mechanisms are
very, very, subtle,

00:48:53.080 --> 00:48:55.680
sort of ostracism is one way of doing it.

00:48:55.960 --> 00:48:57.440
These peculiar mechanisms,

00:48:57.680 --> 00:49:01.800
if someone tries to blow the whistle
you find all sorts of methods.

00:49:02.040 --> 00:49:05.960
You don't just fire them, you give them
far too much work to do,

00:49:06.200 --> 00:49:08.680
you steer them in a different direction.

00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:15.520
One person who is intimately familiar
with how suppression works offshore

00:49:15.760 --> 00:49:19.760
is former Jersey Senator and
Health Minister Stuart Syvret.

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:23.520
In 2009 he leaked a report
on a rouge nurse

00:49:23.760 --> 00:49:27.280
suspected of killing patients
at Jerseys' Hospital.

00:49:27.520 --> 00:49:30.840
Jersey News: It all came to a
head during a States debate

00:49:31.080 --> 00:49:32.560
on Tuesday, 10th of March.

00:49:32.840 --> 00:49:35.840
Senator Stuart Syvret brought
the sitting to a standstill

00:49:36.080 --> 00:49:38.960
claiming Senator Jimmy Perchard
had sworn at him

00:49:39.240 --> 00:49:41.520
and told him to go and top himself.

00:49:41.760 --> 00:49:45.560
Senator Perchard denied the claim
saying, “I absolutely refute that.”

00:49:45.840 --> 00:49:49.320
But less than a week later he
was forced to admit he had lied.

00:49:49.560 --> 00:49:53.360
Senator Perchard has now admitted
that on another occasion he did tell

00:49:53.640 --> 00:49:58.120
Senator Stuart Syvret to go and do
everyone a favour and slit his wrists.

00:49:58.360 --> 00:50:01.080
I was arrested at my house one morning

00:50:01.320 --> 00:50:03.840
by six plainclothes police officers,

00:50:04.080 --> 00:50:08.560
there were another two specialist
data search police officers on hand

00:50:08.840 --> 00:50:12.320
and also another two police
officers in full body armour

00:50:12.560 --> 00:50:16.080
with one of those battering
rams they use in drugs raids.

00:50:16.320 --> 00:50:20.040
So 10 police officers descended
on me without a search warrant,

00:50:20.280 --> 00:50:23.280
the property was turned over
from top to bottom,

00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:26.520
all of the computers were
seized and searched,

00:50:26.760 --> 00:50:30.680
all kinds of private constituency
data was stolen by the police.

00:50:32.080 --> 00:50:36.280
Shortly after we began filming,
a Police officer exited the police station,

00:50:36.520 --> 00:50:40.000
as he hurried passed he
grinned at the camera.

00:50:40.280 --> 00:50:44.120
The officer was on his way to test
the sirens on his motorbike,

00:50:44.360 --> 00:50:49.240
which he did at intervals throughout
much of the remainder of the interview.

00:50:49.480 --> 00:50:52.280
When it came to prosecuting me.

00:50:54.440 --> 00:50:58.240
Stuart Syvret was prosecuted
under the Data Protection Law.

00:50:58.480 --> 00:51:01.280
When the authorities were prosecuting me

00:51:01.520 --> 00:51:05.440
I claimed that this was a legitimate
public interest disclosure defence.

00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:09.360
And I with an expert witness was
able to produce a set of reports

00:51:09.640 --> 00:51:13.120
that effectively destroyed
the prosecution case.

00:51:13.880 --> 00:51:16.880
In reaction to this,
the magistrates ruled

00:51:17.120 --> 00:51:19.200
that Stuart Syvret's defence case,

00:51:19.480 --> 00:51:22.480
that he had leaked the report
in the public interest,

00:51:22.720 --> 00:51:24.920
would no longer be admissible.

00:51:25.160 --> 00:51:29.320
But continued to prosecute him all
the same, without a defence case.

00:51:29.560 --> 00:51:33.920
Over the past seven years Stuart Syvret
has been repeatedly taken to court,

00:51:34.160 --> 00:51:36.160
and imprisoned three times.

00:51:36.400 --> 00:51:40.120
He does not know when or if
the persecution will end,

00:51:40.360 --> 00:51:44.160
nor does he know when he will
find himself behind bars again.

00:51:46.680 --> 00:51:48.920
To look at Jersey from the outside,

00:51:49.160 --> 00:51:51.640
it looks like it has a prosecution system,

00:51:51.920 --> 00:51:55.800
it looks like it has a court and a
legislature, but none of it is real.

00:51:56.040 --> 00:51:58.040
It is a Potemkin village.

00:51:58.320 --> 00:52:02.400
None of these systems in Jersey
meet the tests of being objective

00:52:04.720 --> 00:52:07.120
....or actually
functioning properly.

00:52:07.360 --> 00:52:09.920
The experience that Stuart Syvret had

00:52:11.200 --> 00:52:14.200
is perhaps at the blunter end of the scale.

00:52:16.440 --> 00:52:19.240
He was simply suppressed

00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:22.360
and taken to court on
trumped up charges.

00:52:25.120 --> 00:52:29.200
Let's carry on doing the interview,
they might get bored......

00:52:31.640 --> 00:52:35.120
This is kind of how oppression
happens in Jersey,

00:52:35.360 --> 00:52:38.880
all kinds of little things get
done to harass people.

00:52:39.120 --> 00:52:43.120
They don't often go to the kind of
extremes that they did against me

00:52:43.360 --> 00:52:47.080
in terms of actually arresting me
and putting me in prison.

00:52:47.320 --> 00:52:51.120
The authorities use all kinds of
other lesser little methods

00:52:51.360 --> 00:52:54.880
to interfere with people,
kind of sabotage things,

00:52:55.120 --> 00:52:58.240
obstruct people, make
life difficult for people.

00:52:58.480 --> 00:53:02.360
If you annoy the establishment in
Jersey you won't get a job here,

00:53:02.640 --> 00:53:05.120
your children won’t get decent jobs.

00:53:05.360 --> 00:53:07.880
That is kind of how it works.

00:53:10.680 --> 00:53:14.160
I went to Jersey in March 2009
with Nicholas Shaxson,

00:53:14.400 --> 00:53:17.320
about 24 hours after we
had arrived in Jersey,

00:53:17.560 --> 00:53:20.280
we were there for several days,
Nick said,

00:53:20.520 --> 00:53:23.200
“have you noticed that we
are being followed.

00:53:23.480 --> 00:53:26.960
Ever since we arrived at the
airport we have been followed,

00:53:27.200 --> 00:53:30.320
don't look now but we are
being followed now.”

00:53:30.560 --> 00:53:34.560
It turns out he was quite correct
we were being followed.

00:53:34.800 --> 00:53:38.320
And I found that very disturbing,
very disturbing indeed.

00:53:45.280 --> 00:53:47.600
They have a saying in Jersey,

00:53:47.840 --> 00:53:52.040
"if you don't like it here, there's
always a boat in the morning."

00:53:55.000 --> 00:53:58.000
"Jersey Finance"
promotional literature states,

00:53:58.240 --> 00:54:01.920
"Jersey represents an extension
of the City of London."

00:54:02.200 --> 00:54:06.000
It is where the City of London
chooses to do many of the activities,

00:54:06.240 --> 00:54:09.320
which they couldn't
do in London itself.

00:54:10.400 --> 00:54:13.400
We're here to talk about this
one company called Appleby.

00:54:13.640 --> 00:54:15.920
I’ll just read what they say
on their website.

00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:19.640
“Members of the firm, Appleby,
have gone on not only to political office

00:54:19.920 --> 00:54:22.400
but also in a number of centres,
Bermuda, Jersey,

00:54:22.640 --> 00:54:26.040
the Isle of Man and the Cayman
Islands to senior judicial office.”

00:54:26.320 --> 00:54:28.920
So essentially they are boasting of the fact

00:54:29.160 --> 00:54:32.640
that their staff and their
partners have a real interchange

00:54:32.920 --> 00:54:36.520
between the people that are in
power in these offshore financial centres.

00:54:36.760 --> 00:54:38.960
The same lawyers and accountants

00:54:39.200 --> 00:54:41.920
who set up and administer
offshore trusts,

00:54:42.160 --> 00:54:44.640
also occupy senior political positions.

00:54:44.920 --> 00:54:48.600
In Britain's offshore world,
most politicians are in business,

00:54:48.840 --> 00:54:52.640
they lobby for business and
promote business interests.

00:54:52.920 --> 00:54:55.720
They draft, refine and pass legislation.

00:54:56.960 --> 00:55:01.560
Politicians sit on the boards of the
companies they are supposed to regulate.

00:55:05.240 --> 00:55:08.240
There is no place for dirty money in Britain.

00:55:09.960 --> 00:55:13.760
Indeed there should be no
place for dirty money anywhere.

00:55:14.000 --> 00:55:17.680
The challenge I am laying down
for every country today,

00:55:17.960 --> 00:55:20.440
is to root out the rot of corruption,

00:55:20.680 --> 00:55:24.200
to ensure transparency over what
your own companies are doing,

00:55:24.440 --> 00:55:27.960
and require transparency for foreign
companies in your country too,

00:55:28.200 --> 00:55:32.280
and work with us to spread this approach
to transparency around the world.

00:55:34.240 --> 00:55:36.720
In public British politicians claim

00:55:36.960 --> 00:55:40.880
they are cracking down on
secrecy jurisdictions and corruption,

00:55:41.120 --> 00:55:43.920
but in practice they do the opposite.

00:55:44.760 --> 00:55:47.040
When I talk to politicians in Brussels,

00:55:47.280 --> 00:55:50.880
they say that they have had
more lobbyists from London,

00:55:53.160 --> 00:55:54.840
including politicians,

00:55:55.080 --> 00:55:58.600
come to them to protect the
City of London's interests

00:55:58.840 --> 00:56:02.760
than they have had from every other
European member state combined.

00:56:03.000 --> 00:56:06.480
Which gives you some idea of the
extent to which British politicians

00:56:06.760 --> 00:56:10.240
see themselves as essentially
lobbyists for the City of London.

00:56:10.480 --> 00:56:12.080
We begin with our big story,

00:56:12.360 --> 00:56:14.320
Cayman’s efforts to ensure transparency

00:56:14.560 --> 00:56:17.040
in its financial services sector
is being recognised

00:56:17.320 --> 00:56:19.000
by the international community.

00:56:19.240 --> 00:56:21.760
Earlier this week the U.K.'s
Prime Minister David Cameron

00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:24.920
told Parliament that the
tax haven label placed on

00:56:25.160 --> 00:56:28.640
its Crown Dependencies and
Overseas Territories is unfair.

00:56:31.800 --> 00:56:35.280
Many British politicians have
personal and business ties

00:56:35.520 --> 00:56:39.040
with the City of London and
British secrecy jurisdictions.

00:56:39.280 --> 00:56:42.800
Former British Prime Minister
David Cameron's father, Ian Cameron,

00:56:43.040 --> 00:56:45.520
was an expert in offshore funds,

00:56:45.800 --> 00:56:49.800
and was involved in offshore
trusts from the 1980s onwards.

00:56:50.040 --> 00:56:53.240
At this courthouse behind
me here in St.Helier,

00:56:53.480 --> 00:56:55.000
we found this.

00:56:55.240 --> 00:56:57.720
It is a document called a grant of probate

00:56:58.000 --> 00:57:00.200
and it is attached to the English will of

00:57:00.440 --> 00:57:02.720
Ian Cameron, David Cameron's father.

00:57:05.480 --> 00:57:08.280
Ian Cameron was certainly
a wealthy man,

00:57:08.520 --> 00:57:10.920
in 2009 his personal fortune was estimated

00:57:11.160 --> 00:57:14.840
by researchers for the Sunday
Times Rich list at £10 million.

00:57:15.120 --> 00:57:17.400
Yet when Ian Cameron died in 2010,

00:57:17.640 --> 00:57:20.360
his estate was much smaller
than might be expected,

00:57:20.600 --> 00:57:22.600
just £2.7 million.

00:57:24.280 --> 00:57:25.920
In many cases it is the politicians

00:57:26.160 --> 00:57:28.200
and their cronies and their families and so on,

00:57:28.440 --> 00:57:31.120
and the business people who
sponsor the political parties

00:57:31.360 --> 00:57:33.920
who are using these offshore
services themselves.

00:57:34.160 --> 00:57:36.920
So they have no personal
interest in closing it down.

00:57:37.160 --> 00:57:39.920
If they wanted to close it down,
they could do it tomorrow.

00:57:40.160 --> 00:57:42.920
The fact of the matter is,
they don't want to do it

00:57:43.160 --> 00:57:45.920
because they themselves are
complicit in the process.

00:57:46.160 --> 00:57:48.640
If you come from the same
kind of background

00:57:48.920 --> 00:57:50.920
and you know the right people

00:57:51.160 --> 00:57:53.640
then all the kind of legal
niceties will often fall away.

00:57:53.920 --> 00:57:56.400
You can get away with doing
all sorts of things

00:57:56.640 --> 00:57:59.360
that they wouldn't just let any
old person get away with;

00:57:59.600 --> 00:58:01.520
you know if you came knocking on the door,

00:58:01.760 --> 00:58:04.240
"can you set up an offshore
company to do this?"

00:58:04.520 --> 00:58:07.520
they will tell you to get lost. But,
if you are part of the networks

00:58:07.760 --> 00:58:09.920
you can do these kinds of things.

00:58:10.160 --> 00:58:12.840
That is a very important part
of the whole system,

00:58:13.120 --> 00:58:16.200
this upper class, British public
school kind of establishment

00:58:16.440 --> 00:58:19.240
that has been there for centuries.

00:58:21.680 --> 00:58:25.680
The British establishment, an old
boys network of privileged elites

00:58:25.920 --> 00:58:30.000
had carved out a lucrative niche
for themselves in the offshore world,

00:58:30.280 --> 00:58:32.480
after the demise of empire.

00:58:33.640 --> 00:58:37.120
They transformed themselves
from administrators of empire

00:58:37.360 --> 00:58:39.880
into financial handlers for the global elite

00:58:40.120 --> 00:58:42.120
and multinational corporations.

00:58:42.360 --> 00:58:44.680
As more money flowed offshore,

00:58:44.920 --> 00:58:49.440
societies around the globe began to
feel the impact of the offshore world.

00:58:51.360 --> 00:58:53.920
The reality is not what you believe,

00:58:54.160 --> 00:58:57.640
that your Prime Minister
has the power to decide on

00:58:57.920 --> 00:59:00.400
the future of your country,

00:59:02.280 --> 00:59:04.760
the power is hidden here.

00:59:05.560 --> 00:59:09.240
We have country after
country around the world,

00:59:09.520 --> 00:59:13.400
where the lack of financial
transparency about taxation,

00:59:13.640 --> 00:59:16.160
about ownership, about corruption

00:59:16.400 --> 00:59:19.920
has undermined the extent
to which governments

00:59:20.160 --> 00:59:24.160
deliver representative policy-making
for their citizens.

00:59:24.400 --> 00:59:28.160
We have extreme cases like
the hiding of tax evasion

00:59:28.360 --> 00:59:32.440
by people in finance ministries
in Greece, in France,

00:59:32.640 --> 00:59:37.120
but we also have this system
that in general is geared towards

00:59:37.360 --> 00:59:39.560
anonymous company ownership,

00:59:39.840 --> 00:59:43.320
the anonymous ownership of
properties across London,

00:59:43.560 --> 00:59:46.080
of half of the land in Scotland.

00:59:46.320 --> 00:59:49.840
Do we really think that
there is any circumstance

00:59:50.080 --> 00:59:54.120
in which governments work better,
in which markets work better,

00:59:54.360 --> 00:59:57.760
in which the distribution of
income and assets is better,

00:59:58.000 --> 01:00:00.520
when we allow so much to be hidden?

01:00:00.760 --> 01:00:04.240
Who wants to not know who
you’re doing business with?

01:00:04.520 --> 01:00:08.000
Who wants their government to
have people working for it

01:00:08.240 --> 01:00:11.240
or to be led by people
whose assets are hidden,

01:00:11.520 --> 01:00:15.520
whose financial transactions are
conducted anonymously offshore?

01:00:15.760 --> 01:00:18.240
This is a bad direction for the world.

01:00:18.520 --> 01:00:22.000
We need the citizens to
understand what is happening,

01:00:22.240 --> 01:00:25.760
that they are the ones who
are carrying the burden

01:00:26.000 --> 01:00:28.520
and some individuals with power

01:00:28.760 --> 01:00:32.760
are exonerating themselves
from having to pay tax.

01:00:33.000 --> 01:00:37.000
Ordinary people are paying taxes,
rich people are not,

01:00:37.240 --> 01:00:40.760
so this is inequality and it is
leading up to populism,

01:00:43.000 --> 01:00:44.920
because it shows so clearly,

01:00:45.160 --> 01:00:48.160
that the people leading
the world today

01:00:48.400 --> 01:00:52.920
are not able to take care of the
interests of ordinary people.

01:00:58.960 --> 01:01:00.960
Back in the 1960s and 70s,

01:01:01.200 --> 01:01:04.400
tax evasion or pushing
back against taxation,

01:01:04.640 --> 01:01:07.640
it was kind of seen as
anti-establishment.

01:01:07.880 --> 01:01:11.440
So you know, the Rolling Stones and
Phil Collins, all these people

01:01:11.720 --> 01:01:13.920
kind of going offshore, going away.

01:01:14.160 --> 01:01:17.400
It was seen as kind of a
rebellious thing to do,

01:01:17.640 --> 01:01:20.640
and if you fast-forward
to the present day,

01:01:20.880 --> 01:01:25.400
now that is the establishment,
the offshore system is the establishment.

01:01:25.640 --> 01:01:28.160
Today, offshore is the way elites and

01:01:28.400 --> 01:01:31.880
multinational corporations
conduct their affairs.

01:01:32.160 --> 01:01:35.640
Tax evasion is the way
business is done.

01:01:38.240 --> 01:01:42.720
This kind of sophisticated cheating
requires a huge infrastructure,

01:01:43.920 --> 01:01:47.840
we like to talk about the
pinstriped infrastructure

01:01:48.080 --> 01:01:50.280
of highly educated people

01:01:50.560 --> 01:01:54.360
who think it is their right to
help others to cheat societies.

01:01:55.960 --> 01:01:58.240
We have a new Mafia in town.

01:01:58.480 --> 01:02:00.600
It does not actually shoot people.

01:02:00.840 --> 01:02:03.360
It does not put bullets in their kneecaps.

01:02:03.600 --> 01:02:05.920
But its trade is just as deadly;

01:02:06.160 --> 01:02:09.840
it deprives people of opportunities
to have healthcare,

01:02:10.120 --> 01:02:14.600
education, security, justice and
essentially a fulfilling life.

01:02:17.480 --> 01:02:20.960
Accountants form the backbone
of the offshore system,

01:02:21.200 --> 01:02:25.320
they administer the structures that
allow individuals and corporations

01:02:25.560 --> 01:02:29.080
to shift their money offshore
and evade taxes.

01:02:29.320 --> 01:02:31.320
There are about 2.5 million

01:02:31.560 --> 01:02:34.920
professionally qualified
accountants on this planet,

01:02:35.160 --> 01:02:37.520
about 330,000 are in the UK.

01:02:37.760 --> 01:02:41.480
Well-known people, well dressed,
well fed, highly paid,

01:02:41.720 --> 01:02:43.920
are sitting in city centre offices

01:02:44.160 --> 01:02:47.640
and they are paid to dream up
tax avoidance schemes

01:02:47.920 --> 01:02:50.600
for individuals and for corporations.

01:02:51.720 --> 01:02:54.720
We can all elect a government
which says, "vote for us,"

01:02:54.960 --> 01:02:58.920
we will give you better health care,
better education, better security,

01:02:59.160 --> 01:03:01.320
and the next day the accountant says,

01:03:01.560 --> 01:03:03.720
"sorry folks you elected this government

01:03:03.960 --> 01:03:06.920
but we actually have a
tax avoidance scheme

01:03:07.160 --> 01:03:09.400
and the Amazons and the
Googles and the Microsofts

01:03:09.640 --> 01:03:12.160
won't be paying any
taxes in your jurisdiction.

01:03:12.400 --> 01:03:15.920
Too bad, you voted for it but you're
going to get something else."

01:03:16.160 --> 01:03:17.920
So it is a crazy world,

01:03:18.160 --> 01:03:21.640
that part of the business model
of big accountancy firms

01:03:21.920 --> 01:03:25.920
is how to deny public the services
by erosion of tax revenues.

01:03:26.160 --> 01:03:30.640
And these firms are then rewarded
with government funded contracts,

01:03:30.920 --> 01:03:33.200
and the same firms are then advising

01:03:33.440 --> 01:03:36.960
local governments and
the central government,

01:03:37.200 --> 01:03:41.320
and the same firms then report
on the company accounts

01:03:41.560 --> 01:03:43.720
and tell us all is well.

01:03:43.960 --> 01:03:46.760
When I argued this with a
Price Waterhouse partner

01:03:47.000 --> 01:03:49.120
in a face-to-face debate,
he said

01:03:49.360 --> 01:03:52.240
"Professor Sikka you never
give us credit for anything,

01:03:52.520 --> 01:03:55.400
we generate millions of
dollars of revenues

01:03:55.640 --> 01:03:59.840
and we have lots and lots of satisfied
clients, what is your problem?"

01:04:00.120 --> 01:04:02.400
And my response was very simple,

01:04:02.640 --> 01:04:06.160
"that is the language of
drug pushers and pimps."

01:04:14.400 --> 01:04:18.880
In Britain, a new breed of civil
servant was rising to the top.

01:04:19.120 --> 01:04:22.320
One such civil servant
was Dave Hartnett,

01:04:22.600 --> 01:04:26.080
who rose to the top of HMRC,
the UK tax authority.

01:04:28.680 --> 01:04:32.160
Dave Hartnett had a new
way of collecting tax;

01:04:32.400 --> 01:04:35.920
deals would be negotiated
on an individual basis,

01:04:36.160 --> 01:04:37.880
behind closed doors.

01:04:38.120 --> 01:04:40.600
In the case of the largest clients,

01:04:40.880 --> 01:04:44.360
Dave Hartnett frequently led
the negotiations himself.

01:04:45.600 --> 01:04:49.480
British Telecom was one of the first
companies through the program

01:04:49.720 --> 01:04:52.240
and received a refund of over £1 billion.

01:04:52.480 --> 01:04:55.480
BT’s Chief Executive
Ben Verwaayen wrote:

01:04:55.720 --> 01:04:58.920
“Earnings per share up
14% and nice to know

01:04:59.160 --> 01:05:02.840
that we have a £1 billion
credit from the taxman”.

01:05:05.440 --> 01:05:08.920
Dave Hartnett claimed this
approach was more efficient.

01:05:09.600 --> 01:05:13.080
Litigation in the courts is really
phenomenally expensive

01:05:13.320 --> 01:05:15.840
in this day and age all over the world,

01:05:16.080 --> 01:05:19.200
and I think it is to be
avoided where possible.

01:05:19.440 --> 01:05:21.920
The art is to persuade people to pay

01:05:22.200 --> 01:05:24.920
by strength of argument and the like.

01:05:25.160 --> 01:05:27.920
Pay your tax!
Pay your tax!

01:05:29.440 --> 01:05:31.920
After Protests erupted in 2011,

01:05:32.160 --> 01:05:35.920
the Treasury Select Committee
questioned Dave Hartnett.

01:05:36.160 --> 01:05:40.120
Dave Hartnett claimed he could
not give any information

01:05:40.360 --> 01:05:41.880
due to taxpayer confidentiality.

01:05:43.360 --> 01:05:45.840
What in statute prevents you

01:05:46.080 --> 01:05:48.920
from disclosing information
to Parliament?

01:05:49.160 --> 01:05:53.640
All my advice Mr Barclay so far
has been that I am prevented,

01:05:53.920 --> 01:05:56.200
my colleagues are prevented,

01:05:56.440 --> 01:06:00.560
by the act and by the decision
of the commissioners.

01:06:03.040 --> 01:06:07.520
Dave Hartnett failed to mention that
the legal advice he had received,

01:06:07.760 --> 01:06:10.280
stated that the disclosure of information

01:06:10.520 --> 01:06:13.520
was at the discretion of
the head of HMRC.

01:06:13.760 --> 01:06:17.280
The head of HMRC was
Dave Hartnett himself.

01:06:17.920 --> 01:06:21.240
Just like the Mafia has
penetrated the state,

01:06:21.480 --> 01:06:24.680
accountancy firms have also
penetrated the state.

01:06:24.960 --> 01:06:28.440
The head of anti-avoidance
in the UK tax authority

01:06:28.680 --> 01:06:30.920
is from one of these firms.

01:06:31.160 --> 01:06:35.440
The newly appointed chairman of
her Majesty's revenue and customs,

01:06:35.720 --> 01:06:39.200
which is a tax authority,
is a partner from KPMG.

01:06:39.440 --> 01:06:41.960
Their partners have penetrated the state.

01:06:42.200 --> 01:06:44.720
They are running the Treasury.

01:06:45.840 --> 01:06:49.880
Britain's financial services industry
had penetrated the state.

01:06:50.160 --> 01:06:53.640
And began to shape its laws
for its own benefit.

01:06:55.080 --> 01:06:57.560
The degree of political capture,

01:06:57.800 --> 01:07:01.600
by the City of London, by the big
banks and big law firms

01:07:01.880 --> 01:07:03.360
is so enormous

01:07:03.600 --> 01:07:08.120
that the politicians have effectively
become their spokespersons.

01:07:10.680 --> 01:07:14.560
With the government unwilling to
act in the interest of the public,

01:07:14.800 --> 01:07:19.320
a group of protesters confronted Dave
Hartnett at a private event in Oxford.

01:07:22.920 --> 01:07:27.440
I'm sorry to interrupt. We will just
take a few moments of your time.

01:07:27.680 --> 01:07:31.160
We are here tonight to present
Dave with an award,

01:07:31.440 --> 01:07:35.920
the lifetime achievement award for
services to corporate tax planning.

01:07:42.480 --> 01:07:45.080
Dave has been a great friend
to the industry,

01:07:45.320 --> 01:07:48.000
a great friend to many
of us over the years.

01:07:48.280 --> 01:07:52.240
Really, we just cannot thank you
enough for what you have done.

01:07:52.520 --> 01:07:56.000
From us at Vodafone, it saved us
billions off our tax bill,

01:07:56.280 --> 01:07:59.760
